PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E. Yes, because James reasonably believed that there was an imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm, and disclosure was necessary to prevent the possible harm.

The scenario is governed by the Law Society of Ontario’s Rules of Professional Conduct (r. 3.3-3), which create a narrow exception to confidentiality when a lawyer reasonably believes there is an imminent risk of death or serious bodily harm to an identifiable person or group and disclosure is necessary to prevent that harm. Eric’s explicit statement that he had already purchased a firearm and intended to kill both himself and his ex-wife “tonight” supplies an objectively reasonable basis for James to conclude that the risk was both real and temporally imminent. The information James relayed (names, addresses, and locations) was limited to what the police needed to locate and intervene with Eric; it therefore satisfies the “necessary to prevent the harm” prong.  

Choice A errs by treating “tonight” as non-imminent. Choice B misstates the rule, which expressly permits disclosure in this narrow circumstance. Choice C is factually unsupported: the details disclosed were directly relevant to locating the threat. Choice D invents a duty to comply with any police request, which the Rules do not contain. Consequently only E correctly applies the exception.}

